# Copy to .env and fill in real values for this deployment. Never commit
# the filled-in .env — only this example file (with placeholders) belongs
# in the repository. See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md for the full new-company
# deployment walkthrough.

# This deployment's own PostgreSQL database. Compass Tools is one
# application deployed once per company — each company gets its own
# dedicated database via this variable, never a shared database. Standard
# Prisma connection-string format.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@host:5432/database_name?sslmode=require"

# Google Sign-In OAuth client (see src/server/auth/google-config.ts). A
# single Google Cloud OAuth app can be shared across every company's
# deployment — add each deployment's own domain to that OAuth app's
# Authorized Redirect URIs in Google Cloud Console rather than creating a
# separate Google Cloud project per company.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com"
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET="your-client-secret"

# Encryption key for Gmail OAuth refresh tokens at rest — AES-256-GCM,
# base64-encoded 32 random bytes. Generate a fresh, unique value per
# deployment (never reuse across companies/environments):
#   openssl rand -base64 32
GMAIL_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""

# This deployment's own public base URL — used to build secure
# customer-facing quote/booking links and email content. On Vercel this can
# usually be left unset (falls back to VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL/
# VERCEL_URL automatically); set explicitly for a custom domain or a
# non-Vercel deployment.
APP_BASE_URL="https://your-company-subdomain.example.com"

# Encryption key for stored card data — AES-256-GCM, base64-encoded 32
# random bytes. Generate a fresh, unique value per deployment:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# See src/server/security/card-encryption.ts's file-level comment: this is
# NOT PCI DSS-appropriate key management on its own — review that file
# before handling real cardholder data in production.
CARD_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""

# Encryption keys for the IP capture vault (IpCapture — see
# src/server/security/ip-encryption.ts) — AES-256-GCM + HMAC-SHA256,
# base64-encoded 32 random bytes each. Generate two SEPARATE fresh values
# per deployment (never reuse across each other or across the keys above):
#   openssl rand -base64 32
IP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""
IP_HASH_KEY=""

# Trusted reverse-proxy mode for client IP resolution (booking-signature
# audit trail — see src/lib/request-ip.ts). Leave UNSET for local
# development. For a Vercel production deployment, set to "vercel" — other
# supported values are "cloudflare", "nginx", "generic", matching whatever
# actually sits in front of this deployment. See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md.
# TRUSTED_PROXY="vercel"

# Pass 33 — DEPRECATED, no longer read by the application. Booking
# submission IP data is retained indefinitely by design; this variable
# used to gate the Reveal action's own authorization by age (never data
# deletion) and now has no effect at all. See src/server/actions/
# booking-security.ts and docs/DEPLOYMENT.md.
# BOOKING_IP_RETENTION_DAYS="90"  # deprecated — no longer has any effect

# Set to "production" only once a real payment vault (KMS/HSM) and MFA/
# step-up-authentication system are wired in for this deployment — see the
# detailed warning in .env. Leave unset for development/staging.
# APP_ENV="development"

# Bearer-token secret for the three /api/cron/* endpoints (leads, tasks,
# sequences) — see each route's own file comment. Left UNSET, those routes
# stay open with no auth check at all (fine for local dev, where nothing
# else can reach localhost). Once deployed anywhere public, set this to a
# random secret and configure your scheduler (Vercel Cron, etc.) to send it
# as `Authorization: Bearer <value>` — Vercel Cron does this automatically
# for any cron job when this env var is set on the project. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# CRON_SECRET=""
